From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: paulneja <207793167+paulneja@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 69/69] fbtft: st7789v panel geometry, settable at runtime

The 240x240 ST7789 module is a 240x240 window onto a 240x320 controller.
Which 240 of the 320 rows are wired to glass is a property of the module's
own wiring, and nothing in fbtft or in the devicetree can express it. This
adds the knobs needed to find out what this particular module wants -- and
to change the answer without reflashing the board.

Four things are unknown until the panel is in front of us:

  1. The row offset. The dominant convention is bottom-aligned, visible
     rows 80..319, which is what Adafruit's library hardcodes for every
     240x240 module (`_rowstart = 320 - height`). A top-aligned module
     wants 0. There is no way to tell from the datasheet.

  2. The MADCTL value. fb_st7789v.c's set_var() sends 0x00 at rotation 0;
     Adafruit sends 0xC0 (MX|MY) for the same module. Changing the offset
     without the matching MADCTL, or the reverse, produces a shifted or
     mirrored image with no way to attribute which change caused it.

  3. Whether colour inversion is right. init_display() ends with an
     unconditional MIPI_DCS_ENTER_INVERT_MODE (HSD20_IPS is 1), which is
     correct for the HSD20 IPS glass the driver was written for and is
     also what Adafruit does -- but it is a one-line flip if it is wrong,
     and an inverted panel is otherwise indistinguishable from a broken
     one.

  4. The column offset, for the same reason as (1) at the other rotation.
     Zero for every module seen so far; here so that a module that needs
     one does not need a second patch.

So each is a devicetree property with a working default, and each is also
a writable sysfs attribute beside the existing `debug` file. The node
carries the values that should be right; the sysfs files are for finding
out which ones are, from the serial console, with `echo`, without
rebuilding and reflashing a kernel that takes hours under emulation.

The devicetree properties are bare names, matching every other fbtft
property: fbtft_property_value() is device_property_read_u32(dev, name,
&val) and there is no vendor prefix anywhere in fbtft_properties_read().

Offsets are applied in set_addr_win(), which is the mechanism fb_ili9163.c
already uses for the same problem (its __OFFSET is 32 or 0 depending on
the glass). fbtft_update_display() calls set_addr_win(par, 0, start_line,
xres - 1, end_line) and then passes the same start_line to write_vmem as a
byte offset into screen_buffer, so shifting the window here shifts the
image and nothing else -- the buffer, the length and the frame layout are
untouched.

The offset is added at rotate 0 and 90 and not at 180 and 270, following
fb_ili9163.c: the MADCTL bits for the higher rotations flip the address
counters, so the same physical rows are reached from the other end and
adding the offset again would double it.

The invert knob lives in fb_st7789v.c rather than beside the others,
because hsd20_ips is that file's own variable. The core gets it there
through a pair of optional hooks, display->sysfs_init and ->sysfs_exit,
which fbtft_sysfs_init()/exit() call after creating the geometry knobs. A
driver that sets neither gets no extra attributes, so nothing changes for
the other fbtft drivers. The hook exists rather than a plain pointer to
the flag because a pointer taken in a static initialiser is a compile-time
constant, and GCC warns about that under -Waddress, which this tree builds
with -Werror.

The geometry writes take effect on the next redraw, and schedule one, so a
write is visible immediately rather than at the next thing that happens to
dirty a line. The invert write takes effect at the next init_display(),
which is where the command is sent.

Signed-off-by: paulneja <207793167+paulneja@users.noreply.github.com>
